<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  android:accessibilityEventTypes is deliberately ABSENT. The attribute defaults
  to 0, so the service subscribes to nothing and onAccessibilityEvent() is never
  called. AutoScrollService.onServiceConnected() also sets eventTypes = 0
  explicitly at runtime so the property is visible in code, not just by omission.

  android:canRetrieveWindowContent="false" is the load-bearing line. With it the
  platform never binds window content to this service: getRootInActiveWindow(),
  getWindows() and friends return null/empty regardless of what the code asks
  for. The privacy claim is enforced by the OS, not by us remembering not to call
  those methods. Do not flip this to true.

  android:isAccessibilityTool="true" is a FUNCTIONAL requirement, not a
  submission tactic:

    - It routes the Play declaration to the accessibility-tool branch, which asks
      whether the app genuinely serves disabled users rather than whether it is
      dangerous.
    - It carries the exemption from Play's autonomy prohibition (policy
      effective 28 Jan 2026).
    - Android has been testing an Advanced Protection Mode change that blocks
      accessibility permission grants to apps WITHOUT the flag, and revokes it
      from installed ones. If that ships, this line is what makes the app
      installable for its users at all.

  API 30+; older platforms ignore it, so it is safe against minSdk 26. It is a
  one-line attribute that a merge can drop silently, which is why the production
  CI ratchet must assert its presence from the built artifact — alongside
  capabilities == 32 and the absence of INTERNET.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:canPerformGestures="true"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="false"
    android:description="@string/service_description"
    android:isAccessibilityTool="true"
    android:notificationTimeout="0" />
